Z||000000||DXR_WILL||07-12-99||11:30 PM||||WHILE DRIVING MY DADS FIERO I HAVE NOTICED THAT HIS BRAKES ARE KIND OF WEAK (MY DAD SAYS THERE JUST FINE).  IT SEEMS TO ME THAT THE BRAKES ON THE FIERO CAN NOT STOP THE FIERO FAST ENOUGH.  CAN ANYONE HELP ME OUT WITH INFO ON BRAKE UPGRADES AND SO FORTH.  
Z||000001||PaUL||07-13-99||12:11 AM||||Its not that the brakes cant stop the car fast enough, its that the pedal effort is high. If you stomp on the brake pedal, it will stop fast enough. It could be because of the long length of the vacuum line to the brake booster. It has to go all the way from the intake manifold to the front. 
Z||000002||batboy||07-13-99||08:58 PM||||I think it's generally accepted that the Fiero brakes are adequate under normal driving conditions, but are somewhat marginal under performance driving conditions. The two main reasons: 1) thin solid rotors that get hot when used hard and this could cause brake fade, and 2) the calipers have fairly small piston diameters. There are two or three fairly recent discussions about brakes here in the forum. A swap to thicker, vented rotors and larger calipers is being done by many Fiero owners who demand better brakes. There are a number of companies on the web that offer complete brake upgrades in the form of a kit, like Ryane Motor Sports is one example of a really hi-fi brake setup: http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/fiero.htm <p>A popular driveway mechanic brake swap is the late 1980's Grand Am/Baretta front brakes on both the front and back of the Fiero. There is some machine shop work that must be done and some grinding here and there to modify everything, plus you lose your e-brake. This link tells about it:  http://members.tripod.com/Randy_Agee/brakes2.html
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||07-13-99||09:41 PM||||DXR_WILL, Stop driving my car at 80-90 MPH all the time and you won`t have a brake problem.<p>P.S. You are more than welcome to do the upgrades if you want!!
